WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Hooks: the checks and balances that always run.

2
00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:11.437
Hooks run your command (or an HTTP call, an MCP tool, or a model prompt) at fixed points in Claude Code’s lifecycle.

3
00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:15.003
They live in a hooks block in a settings file: ~/.

4
00:00:15.283 --> 00:00:16.563
claude/settings.

5
00:00:16.843 --> 00:00:18.976
json for you on every project,.

6
00:00:19.256 --> 00:00:20.536
claude/settings.

7
00:00:20.816 --> 00:00:23.739
json for the repository (committed and shared),.

8
00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:25.299
claude/settings.

9
00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:26.368
local.

10
00:00:26.648 --> 00:00:31.171
json for you on this project, or managed settings for an organization.

11
00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:34.928
Skills and subagents can also carry hooks in their frontmatter.

12
00:00:35.208 --> 00:00:50.547
The configuration has three levels: an event such as PreToolUse, a list of matcher groups that filter when it fires (for tool events, a pattern on the tool name like Edit|Write or Bash ), and handlers to run, usually {"type": "command", "command": "."

13
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:52.811
} with an optional timeout in seconds.

14
00:00:53.091 --> 00:01:07.619
Your command receives the event as JSON on stdin (including tool_name and tool_input for tool events) and replies with its exit code: 0 for no objection, 2 to block or push back with a message on stderr.

15
00:01:07.899 --> 00:01:10.565
For richer control it can print JSON instead.

16
00:01:10.845 --> 00:01:15.624
What exit code 2 does depends on the event, and getting this right is the whole game.

17
00:01:15.904 --> 00:01:19.360
From the hooks reference: PreToolUse: blocks the tool call.

18
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.861
Use it to protect paths or refuse dangerous commands.

19
00:01:23.141 --> 00:01:28.688
PostToolUse: can’t block, because the tool already ran; it shows your stderr to Claude.

20
00:01:28.968 --> 00:01:32.595
Use it to feed back lint or test failures right after an edit.

21
00:01:32.875 --> 00:01:36.757
Stop: prevents Claude from stopping and continues the conversation.

22
00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:40.109
Use it as a gate: “you’re not done until the tests pass.

23
00:01:40.389 --> 00:01:40.687
”.

24
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:44.423
UserPromptSubmit: blocks the prompt before Claude sees it.

25
00:01:44.703 --> 00:01:51.167
The best-practices doc’s guidance on which hooks to write is short: a hook is for a rule with zero exceptions.

26
00:01:51.447 --> 00:01:54.092
“Never edit the generated client” is a hook.

27
00:01:54.372 --> 00:01:58.383
“Prefer named exports” has plenty of exceptions and stays in CLAUDE.

28
00:01:58.663 --> 00:01:59.474
md.

29
00:01:59.754 --> 00:02:08.436
For real enforcement use command or http handlers; prompt and agent hooks ask a model to decide, so they aren’t deterministic.
